--TEST--
Core\Random::int's closed bound is drawn at the ends of `int` itself — a singleton at each extreme, the whole 2^64-value range, and the two-value ranges pinned against each end — spec § 11, ADR 0007 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The standing sweep asks for `int(1, 6)` and for a range of 2^62 either side
// of zero. What none of them asks is the bound of the *type*: the member is
// inclusive at both ends, so `int(min, min)` and `int(max, max)` each name one
// value, and `int(min, max)` names a range of 2^64 values whose width has no
// `int` at all. An implementation computing `$max - $min + 1` is fine at 2^63
// and overflows here.

// ADR 0007 § 4: an integer literal past `int` is a diagnostic, so the lower
// end is written as the negation of the largest one plus a step.
int $lo = -9223372036854775807 - 1;
int $hi = 9223372036854775807;
echo "ends ", $lo, " ", $hi, "\n";

// --- A singleton at each end ----------------------------------------------
// `int($n, $n)` is `$n` (the member's own doc), and that stays true where `$n`
// is the first and last value the type holds. These two lines are exact, not
// a sweep: there is one answer.
echo "min singleton ", Core\Random::int($lo, $lo), "\n";
echo "max singleton ", Core\Random::int($hi, $hi), "\n";

// --- The whole type as one range ------------------------------------------
// 2^64 values. Counted rather than read off a line, so a draw that clamps or
// wraps at one end fails here while still looking plausible on any single row.
int $whole = 0;
int $i = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 64; $i = $i + 1) {
    int $w = Core\Random::int($lo, $hi);
    $whole = $whole + ((($w >= $lo) && ($w <= $hi)) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "whole ", $whole, "\n";

// --- Two values, hard against each end ------------------------------------
// The narrowest range that is not a singleton, placed where an off-by-one has
// nowhere to hide: `{min, min+1}` and `{max-1, max}`. Both endpoints must be
// reachable — over 48 draws each, missing one is under 1 in 10^14 — and no
// third value may appear, which is what an implementation that computed its
// span one too wide would produce.
int $lowPair = 0;
int $sawLo = 0;
int $sawLoUp = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 48; $i = $i + 1) {
    int $l = Core\Random::int($lo, $lo + 1);
    $lowPair = $lowPair + ((($l == $lo) || ($l == $lo + 1)) ? 1 : 0);
    $sawLo = $sawLo + (($l == $lo) ? 1 : 0);
    $sawLoUp = $sawLoUp + (($l == $lo + 1) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "low pair ", $lowPair, " both ", (($sawLo > 0) && ($sawLoUp > 0)) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

int $highPair = 0;
int $sawHi = 0;
int $sawHiDown = 0;
int $j = 0;
for ($j = 0; $j < 48; $j = $j + 1) {
    int $h = Core\Random::int($hi - 1, $hi);
    $highPair = $highPair + ((($h == $hi) || ($h == $hi - 1)) ? 1 : 0);
    $sawHi = $sawHi + (($h == $hi) ? 1 : 0);
    $sawHiDown = $sawHiDown + (($h == $hi - 1) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "high pair ", $highPair, " both ", (($sawHi > 0) && ($sawHiDown > 0)) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// --- The first refused range, at the same extremes ------------------------
// `$min > $max` is the empty range and throws rather than swapping the bounds.
// One step past each singleton is the first refusal, and the message carries
// both numbers so a caller can see which computed bound was wrong.
try {
    Core\Random::int($lo + 1, $lo);
    echo "low empty drew\n";
} catch (Throwable $lowEmpty) {
    echo $lowEmpty->message, "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Random::int($hi, $hi - 1);
    echo "high empty drew\n";
} catch (Throwable $highEmpty) {
    echo $highEmpty->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
ends -9223372036854775808 9223372036854775807
min singleton -9223372036854775808
max singleton 9223372036854775807
whole 64
low pair 48 both y
high pair 48 both y
Core\Random::int(): the range is empty — the lower bound -9223372036854775807 is above the upper bound -9223372036854775808, and both ends are inclusive
Core\Random::int(): the range is empty — the lower bound 9223372036854775807 is above the upper bound 9223372036854775806, and both ends are inclusive
